<h1>Judah Halevi</h1><div id="pubinfo"><em>First published Thu Jul 30, 2026</em></div>

<div id="preamble">

<!--pdf exclude begin-->

<p class="smaller">
[<em>Editor&rsquo;s Note: The following new entry by Ehud Krinis
replaces the
 <a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2026/entries/halevi/">former entry</a>
 on this topic by the previous author.</em>]</p>

<!--pdf exclude end-->

<p>
Judah Halevi (died 1141) was a Jewish poet, thinker, physician, and
public figure. He was one of the exemplary figures of the period known
as the &ldquo;Golden Age of Spanish Jewry.&rdquo; He lived and worked
in the Iberian peninsula during the final decades of the eleventh
century and the first decades of the twelfth century. He is considered
one of the greatest Hebrew poets of all time (Gil and Fleischer 2001,
17; Scheindlin 2008, 5&ndash;6). He is also known as the author of a
book whose long title is <em>Book of Refutation and Proof on Behalf of
the Despised Religion</em> (<em>Kit&#257;b al-radd
wa&#702;l-dal&#299;l f&#299;&#702;l-d&#299;n al-dhal&#299;l</em>), but
which has been known to its readers throughout the generations as
<em>The Book of the Kuzari</em> or simply <em>The Kuzari</em> (after
the Hebrew namesake of the book&rsquo;s Khazar king character). In
accordance with the conventions of the specific Jewish culture in
which he active, Judah Halevi composed this scholarly work in
Judeo-Arabic (Arabic written in Hebrew characters, interspersed with
citations and words from Jewish tradition in their original
languages). <em>The Kuzari</em> is a unique work unlike any other in
Jewish thought. In its standard pre-modern Hebrew translation made by
Judah ibn Tibbon (d. c. 1190), it became one of the most widespread
and influential works in Jewish theology (Shear 2008). Halevi was the
premier formulator of Jewish fideism vis-&agrave;-vis rationalistic
trends in general and the philosophical trend in particular. He
distinguishes himself in employing skeptical philosophical measures to
support his fideistic attitude (Arieli 1988; Krinis 2020).</p>
</div> 

