<h2 id="Biog">1. Biography</h2>

<p>
Only a few details are known about most of Judah Halevi&rsquo;s life.
His birth year is unknown. Circumstantial evidence suggests that he
was born during the eighth decade or the first half of the ninth
decade of the eleventh century (Scheindlin 2008, 15, 254) . His
birthplace was likely the city of Tudela in the northwest of the
Iberian peninsula. Various hints embedded in the poems of Jewish poets
of the period suggest he grew up in the northern, Christian regions of
the Iberian peninsula (Schirmann 1979, 251&ndash;253).</p>

<p>
While still in his youth, Halevi&rsquo;s reputation as a poet of
exceptional talent was already established. He continued to write
poetry throughout his life and was a prolific poet. Although, due to
his upbringing in the Christian north, he was initially considered an
outsider in the Jewish Andalusian social elite, his early talent as a
poet earned him an entry ticket to the circles of this elite (Brann
2000b, 268&ndash;269). Members of this Jewish-Andalusian elite adopted
the values and curriculum of the dominant Islamicate elite culture of
their time; some even served in various positions in centers of power
and government in the Christian and Muslim kingdoms of the Iberian
peninsula.</p>

<p>
While he was living in the town of Lucena, Judah Halevi acquired
knowledge of Jewish law from the great Jewish-Andalusian rabbis of his
generation. Despite his rabbinic education and the title
&ldquo;Rabbi&rdquo; which was attributed to him, he did not function
as a Rabbi in any Jewish community. His primary livelihood came from
his work as a physician. Among other places, he worked in this
profession in the city of Toledo, during the period when control of
this central city passed into Christian hands. In a later period of
his life, Halevi lived for years in the city of C&oacute;rdoba,
another central city in the Iberian peninsula, which was still under
Muslim rule when Halevi lived there (Yahalom 2009, 51&ndash;80).</p>

<p>
The last years of Halevi&rsquo;s life were his most prolific and
significant. In these years, he wrote many of his famous &ldquo;Zion
poems,&rdquo; as well as <em>The Kuzari</em>. Finally, he also
succeeded in realizing his aspiration to reach the Land of Israel and
die there.</p>

<p>
Our knowledge of this late period is much richer than our knowledge of
the preceding periods of Halevi&rsquo;s life. The main source of this
knowledge is surviving letters that have reached us from fragments of
documents, books, and papers that were piled up for centuries in a
room of a synagogue in old Cairo. Following the transfer of this
written material to libraries in Western countries, it became known as
the &ldquo;Cairo Geniza.&rdquo;</p>

<p>
The relevant letters from the Cairo Geniza are being gradually
uncovered and deciphered by various scholars. They are all connected
to the Jewish-Egyptian merchant Halfon ben Nethanel, who became
acquainted with Halevi in the last years of his life. They include
information about Halevi&rsquo;s connections with his circle of
acquaintances and some of his public and economic activities. These
letters also contain extensive information about Halevi&rsquo;s
journey from the Iberian peninsula to the Land of Israel. This journey
included a stopover in Egypt, where he stayed for about eight months
(early September 1140 to mid-May 1141). During this stay, Judah Halevi
was hosted by key figures in the Jewish communities of Alexandria and
Cairo. In May 1141, Halevi sailed from the port of Alexandria on his
way to the Land of Israel. The Cairo Geniza letters discovered and
deciphered so far do not include specific information about his fate
in the Land of Israel, but it can be inferred from them that he died
there in the summer of 1141, about two months after his departure from
Alexandria (Scheindlin 2008, 97&ndash;152).</p>

<p>
The most recent scholarly synthesis of the relevant Cairo Geniza
findings presented by M. A. Friedman, and based on additional letters
that were not accessible previously, paints a different picture from
the one accepted by earlier scholars. According to the earlier
scholars most prominently S. D. Goitein, M. Gil and E. Fleisher), the
writing of <em>The Kuzari</em> took many years, during which the book
underwent several changes and edits (Goitein 1988. Gil and Fleisher
2001, 182&ndash;184). In contrast, according to the picture emerging
from the more up-to-date research, Halevi carried out the writing of
<em>The Kuzari</em> in a much shorter period, in the few years that
preceded his departure for his journey to the Land of Israel in 1140.
This conclusion is based on a new dating of the Cairo Geniza letters
that refer to the initial acquaintance between Halevi and Halfon ben
Nethanel, including a letter in which Halevi refers briefly and
enigmatically to the circumstances of <em>The Kuzari</em>&rsquo;s
writing. Recent studies have deduced that these letters were written
during a visit by Halfon to the Iberian peninsula that took place in
1138&ndash;1139. This dating of the time of Halevi and Halfon ben
Netanel&rsquo;s initial acquaintance is considerably later than the
dates attributed to it in previous studies (Friedman 2013,
226&ndash;253; Friedman 2016; Lasker 2024).</p>

<h2 id="LiteStruKuzaSociBack">2. The Literary Structure of <em>The Kuzari</em> and its Social Background</h2>

<p>
The opening remarks of <em>The Kuzari</em> indicate that it belongs to
the genre of polemical literature. It includes a polemic directed at
philosophers, an inter-religious polemic directed at Christians and
Muslims, and an intra-Jewish polemic directed at the Karaites as a
minority group among the Jews.</p>

<p>
From a literary standpoint, the prominent features of <em>The
Kuzari</em> are its dialogue and frame story components (Schweid 1970,
38&ndash;44; Silman 1995, 331&ndash;338 Hughes, 2007, 481&ndash;489;
Ravitsky 2019, 554&ndash;558). The dialogues in the book are related
to its frame story. The dialogues occupy the lion&rsquo;s share of the
book&rsquo;s five treatises, while segments of the frame story are
integrated into the opening of the first treatise, the opening of the
second treatise, and the end of the fifth and last treatise. The
book&rsquo;s plot takes place in the court of an anonymous king,
identified as the ruler of the Khazar kingdom. The time of the plot is
four hundred years before Halevi wrote <em>The Kuzari</em>, that is in
the year 4500 of the Jewish calendar, which corresponds to parts of
the years 739&ndash;740 of the Christian calendar (1:1, 1:47).</p>

<p>
At the center of the plot is a repeated dream of the Khazar king. The
message in the dream, conveyed by an angelic figure, undermines the
Khazar king&rsquo;s confidence in his specific religion. Subsequently,
the Khazar king summons several sages to his court, to discuss their
doctrines with him. In the first stage, the invitees are a philosopher
and two sages representing the Christian and Islamic religions. In the
second stage, after the discussions with these three sages have not
satisfied him, the Khazar king also invites another representative
called <em>&#7716;abr</em> (Heb. <em>&#7716;aver</em>) which is an
Arabic appellation of a Jewish sage. The dialogues between the Khazar
king and the philosopher and between the king and the sages of
Christianity and Islam are brief and occupy a relatively small part of
the book&rsquo;s first treatise (1:1&ndash;9). In contradistinction,
the dialogue between the Khazar king and the Jewish sage is long and
detailed. It spans most of the remainder of the book and spreads over
its five treatises.</p>

<p>
Despite the general similarity of <em>The Kuzari</em> to other works
in the dialogical genre of its period, some of its features are
exceptional. One important difference is that the dialogue that takes
place between the Khazar king and the Jewish sage throughout most of
the book does not conform to the common imbalance between the two
speakers, where one serves as the guide and the other as the guided.
The Jewish sage is indeed the dominant figure and enjoys an advantage
over his interlocutor, the king. Nevertheless, occasionally the king
often proves to be a valuable interlocutor. In few places throughout
the dialogue, he even functions as an equal (5:7&ndash;8), and
sometimes even points out weaknesses in the Jewish sage&rsquo;s
arguments (1:114; 2:23; 4:26).</p>

<p>
Another related difference that distinguishes <em>The
Kuzari</em>&rsquo;s dialogue from the conventional dialogical pattern
is that in works of that kind, the aim of the dialogical form is that
the participant in the dialogue who does not share the author&rsquo;s
denomination will recognize the superiority and pre-eminence of the
arguments presented by the speaker representing the author&rsquo;s
denomination. This recognition may culminate in the
interlocutor&rsquo;s conversion to his speaker&rsquo;s denomination
and joining his group. In contrast, <em>The Kuzari</em>&rsquo;s
narrative concludes long after the Khazar king&rsquo;s conversion to
Judaism, with the Jewish sage&rsquo;s decision to leave the
king&rsquo;s court and travel to the Land of Israel. The Khazar king
objects to his interlocutor&rsquo;s plan and argues strongly against
the claims the latter raises to justify it. Against this backdrop, the
two debaters separate as friends, to be sure, but not as those who
have reached an agreement (5:22&ndash;28).</p>

<p>
Ostensibly, the frame story that Halevi has crafted is based, as he
admits in the opening, on known facts from historical sources.
According to these sources, in a period preceding the writing of
<em>The Kuzari</em> by several centuries, the people of the Khazar
kingdom, all or some of them, converted to Judaism following an
inter-religious encounter in which their king debated with the
religious sages of Christianity, Islam, and Judaism (Golden, 2007;
Wasserstein, 2007).</p>

<p>
Yet Halevi allows himself to deviate from the narratives appearing in
these sources, in ways that indicate that his connection to them is a
free and non-binding connection (Levinger 1971, 476&ndash;480). The
prominent expression of this deviation by Halevi is the way he sets
not the Judaization of the Khazar king and his people, but rather the
journey to the Land of Israel, as the final goal to which his
book&rsquo;s frame story aspires. Another notable deviation is the
inclusion of the figure of the philosopher in the dialogues conducted
by the Khazar king. In the previous Jewish and Muslim sources of the
story of the conversion of the Khazars and their king, there is no
mention of the figure of the philosopher, whereas in Halevi&rsquo;s
book, he appears as the first of the speakers summoned by the Khazar
king to debate with him in his court.</p>

<p>
The notable deviations of <em>The Kuzari</em>&rsquo;s author from the
narrative of the Judaization of the Khazar king and his subjects, as
it was known to contemporary readers, can be understood in light of
aspects in Halevi&rsquo;s own life that motivated him to compose his
book. As mentioned above, <em>The Kuzari</em> was written close to the
time of Halevi&rsquo;s departure for his journey to the Land of
Israel. The preparations that the author made for this journey at that
time were known to the people in his circle within the
Jewish-Andalusian elite. It is against this backdrop that the details
of <em>The Kuzari</em>&rsquo;s frame story acquire their full meaning
as a cover story as I shall explain below.</p>

<p>
The development of the book&rsquo;s plot in the court of the Khazar
king alludes to the courtly environment in which the culture of the
Jewish-Andalusian elite of the period developed and flourished. The
services that the members of this elite provided in the courts of
rulers, powerful men, and wealthy patrons of science and culture (some
of whom were also Jews), in the Muslim and Christian cities and
kingdoms, brought them into direct contact with the human, religious
and intellectually diverse world of the courtly culture in the Iberian
peninsula (Brann 2000a). From Halevi&rsquo;s perspective, as one who
knew the Jewish-Andalusian elite from within, the participation and
close contacts of the Jewish elite in the cultural world of foreign
rulers&rsquo; courts were perceived as a serious threat to the
identity and stability of the Jewish communities scattered in exile.
Thus, as a cover story, the main point of <em>The Kuzari</em>&rsquo;s
frame story is to points to the danger that the exile of the people of
Israel poses to its existence. This danger is especially acute in the
specific manifestation of this exile in the inter-religious and
multi-directional encounter and assimilation of the Jewish-Andalusian
elite with the courtly culture of their time and place (Berger,
1992).</p>

<p>
Throughout the book, views uttered by Khazar king tend to resemble
views typical of those of the educated members of the
Jewish-Andalusian elite. The individuals that the king first summons
to his court&mdash;the philosopher, the Christian sage, and the Muslim
sage &mdash; are prototypes of individuals whom the members of the
Jewish-Andalusian elite encountered as part of their activities in the
courts of the wealthy and powerful rulers and patrons. The polemical
tone of <em>The Kuzari</em> is revealed to be primarily directed at
the views and values of this elite. The danger posed by the daily
rubbing shoulders of the Jewish elite with the corresponding Christian
and Muslim elites in the courts of the Andalusian power brokers is
expressed in the counterarguments of the Jewish sage against the
claims of the representatives of these religions. Furthermore, the
unusual inclusion of the figure of the philosopher at the beginning of
the frame story, the extensive treatment of the philosophers and their
views by the Khazar king and his Jewish interlocutor, and the polemic
against these views throughout the book, all testify to the great
interest and special attraction that the Jewish-Andalusian elite
showed toward philosophy (Cohen 1967, 295&ndash;302).</p>

<p>
Finally, the Jewish sage&rsquo;s departure from the Khazar
king&rsquo;s court on his way to the Land of Israel, at the end of the
book (5:22&ndash;28) corresponds to Halevi&rsquo;s own decision to
dedicate the rest of his life to the realization of a vision of self
and national redemption. The fact that Halevi&rsquo;s peers in the
Jewish-Andalusian elite rejected this voyage and its justifications,
and declined to join it, is reflected in some of Halevi&rsquo;s late
poems (Alfonso 2008, 78&ndash;82), as well as in the counterarguments
he places in the mouth of the Khazar king (5:22; 5:24; 5:26).</p>

<h2 id="IdenPhilPhilSubjCritKuza">3. The Identity of the Philosophy and Philosophers Subject to Critique in <em>The Kuzari</em></h2>

<p>
Halevi placed the plot of <em>The Kuzari</em> four hundred years
before the time of his composition of the work. Hence, the
book&rsquo;s plot takes place in a period that followed the emergence
and rise of Islam but by large precedes the emergence of philosophy
written by philosophers of the Islamicate cultural world. Accordingly,
<em>The Kuzari</em>, as a work written in Judeo-Arabic, lacks any
explicit reference to philosophical literature written in Arabic,
whether by Muslim authors or by Christian and Jewish authors. With the
curious exception of the astronomer al-Batt&#257;n&#299; (4:29), none
of the writers of the Islamicate period are mentioned in the book.</p>

<p>
Throughout <em>The Kuzari</em>, philosophy is considered the legacy of
ancient Greek culture. The names of philosophers and philosophical
schools mentioned in it are always those of figures and schools from
the Greek and Hellenistic periods (1:1; 4:25; 5:14). The same is true
for the few philosophical books explicitly mentioned (4:25; 5:8).</p>

<p>
Nevertheless, signs emerge between the lines that testify to the close
connection of the book and its author to the world of philosophy of
his time. For example, Aristotle is referred to simply as &lsquo;the
philosopher&rsquo; (1:73) &ndash; the status accorded to him within
the culture of the Muslim period. Furthermore, in one place in the
book, the Jewish sage distinguishes between &ldquo;the philosophers of
Greece,&rdquo; who had no acquaintance with the prophets and their
miracles, and &ldquo;those who philosophize among the followers of the
religions,&rdquo; whose doctrines are already marked by this
acquaintance (4:3 in comparison with 1:63; 1:65). These statements
express the author&rsquo;s understanding that the encounter and
tension between philosophy and revealed religions belongs to the
post-Greek period. Thus, it was clear to him that the challenge posed
by philosophy to these religions, and to the religion of Israel in
particular, is a matter whose relevance concerns precisely the
development of philosophy in periods later than the period of
classical Greek philosophy.</p>

<p>
It is not surprising, therefore, that contrary to the impression
Halevi attempts to create, according to which the philosophy with
which he contends and polemicizes is Greek philosophy, an examination
of the philosophical doctrines presented in <em>The Kuzari</em>
reveals that in most cases, their formulations are based on the legacy
of philosophers of his own period and culture. Although the connection
of these philosophers to the Greek and Hellenistic philosophical
legacies was strong, those prominent among them, such as al-Farabi (d.
950 or 951) and Ibn Sina (d. 1037), were original philosophers who
added their own contributions to the classical legacy on which they
relied.</p>

<p>
In the presentation of the anonymous philosopher to the Khazar king on
the core positions of the philosophers (1:1), only the names of Greek
philosophers are mentioned. Nevertheless, the examination of the
philosophers&rsquo; doctrines presented in this section as well as
other sections in the book, reveals that they are based on the
doctrines of the great Arab philosophers of the
period&mdash;al-Farabi, Ibn Sina, and Ibn Bajja (d. 1139)&mdash;as
well as the formulations of the great theologian al-Ghazali (d. 1111),
which highlight the tension between the doctrines of al-Farabi and Ibn
Sina and the core principles of the revealed religions (Kaufmann 1877,
119&ndash;140; Baneth 1941&ndash;2 [1981]; Kogan 2002). The theory of
the Separate Intellects and the special functions reserved in it to
the Active Intellect (the lowest among those Intellects), that the
Jewish sage attributes to Aristotle in one place (4:25). is a theory
developed by al-Farabi and adopted with modifications Ibn Sina and Ibn
Bajja (Davidson 1972, 352&ndash;381; Davidson, 1992, 181&ndash;195;
Pines 1980, 210&ndash;217). The philosophical theory of prophecy that
the Jewish sage sharply polemicizes against (1:87) is a doctrine of
al-Farabi (Lobel 2000, 140&ndash;141; Krinis 2013, 5). The
philosophical theory of the soul that the Jewish sage presents in
detail (5:12) is a doctrine of Ibn Sina. This case is especially
illuminating, as the presentation of this subject that Halevi puts in
the mouth of the Jewish sage in this case is an abridged paraphrase of
a treatise by Ibn Sina (Landauer, 1872, 335&ndash;336).</p>

<p>
We learn that just as <em>The Kuzari</em>&rsquo;s frame story is
primarily a cover story, in which the events in the Khazar
king&rsquo;s court in the eighth century serve as a literary disguise
for Halevi&rsquo;s engagement with and reaction to the courtly culture
of his time, so too his reference to philosophy as an ancient Greek
legacy is a disguise for his engagement and reaction to the philosophy
of his own time.</p>

<h2 id="FideFramForCritPhilKuza">4. Fideism as the Framework for the Critique of Philosophy in <em>The Kuzari</em></h2>

<p>
Rhetorically, the Jewish sage&rsquo;s and the Khazar king&rsquo;s
statements about the philosophers throughout the dialogues of <em>The
Kuzari</em> are marked by a lack of uniformity. On the one hand, they
use harsh expressions of condemnation. For example, the Khazar king
points out that Aristotle and his followers advocate a position that
attributes wisdom and an active capacity for action to nature. In
doing so, he believes, they are guilty of the grave sin, from a
religious standpoint, of association, i.e., attributing to a created
force capabilities belonging only to God (1:76). Similarly, in the
opinion of the Jewish sage, one who follows the philosophical
cosmological theory, according to which God&rsquo;s influence on the
existing world is mediated by a hierarchy of intermediate entities,
should be considered a heretic (5:21).</p>

<p>
On the other hand, the Jewish sage shows appreciation for the
philosophers by admitting that among those who use methods of rational
inference, they are the most meticulous and serious (4:3; 4:15).
Therefore, their achievements in what is accessible to rational
inquiry are the most impressive (1:99).</p>

<p>
This lack of uniformity in rhetorical expressions toward the
philosophers is largely explained by understanding that Halevi&rsquo;s
general approach to philosophy is consistent with a position that in
the modern period is referred to as fideism. Generally speaking, a
fideistic approach is one that emphasizes the independence and
autonomy of religious faith from reason (Amesbury, 2022). Furthermore,
rational inquiry is perceived within the fideistic conception as
unsuitable as a basis for religious faith. Halevi&rsquo;s fideism is
based not on an extreme anti-rationalist position that denies any
value to rational inquiry, but on a moderate and selective fideistic
position. According to him, philosophical rational inquiry does yield
valid results in certain fields, but these fields are not the ones
with important religious-theological implications.</p>

<p>
In the programmatic formulation of this position, the Jewish sage
points to the difference between the realms of &ldquo;human
wisdom&rdquo; and &ldquo;divine wisdom&rdquo; (Halper 2021,
69&ndash;77). &ldquo;Human wisdom&rdquo; is the realm that includes
the theory of logic and the mathematical sciences, fields in which the
philosophers, as those most dedicated to rational inquiry, have
achieved undeniable accomplishments. However, in the higher sciences,
those of physics and metaphysics, the philosophers have failed to
achieve similar decisive results. This failure, says the Jewish sage,
is unavoidable, since those lacking the divine light of prophecy, like
philosophers, lack the ability to establish certain conclusions in the
sciences that relate to the realm of &ldquo;divine wisdom&rdquo;
(5:14).</p>

<p>
Halevi&rsquo;s moderate and selective fideism is further expressed in
the issue of providing rationalistic explanations for the religious
commandments. On this matter, he distinguishes between &ldquo;rational
and governmental commandments&rdquo; and &ldquo;divine auditory
commandments.&rdquo; According to the Jewish sage, the &ldquo;rational
commandments,&rdquo; those that can be explicated by means of reason,
were included in the Torah of Israel because they are binding rules
for any human society. In contrast, the epistemological status of the
&ldquo;divine commandments,&rdquo; such as the ones related to the
Temple&rsquo;s sacrifices, Sabbath and circumcision, commandments
which are unique to the people of Israel, is neutral. Reason neither
approves nor disapproves them (2:48). Therefore, any rational
explanations we can offer to clarify them cannot be considered
satisfactory and decisive explanations (2:26; 2:60).</p>

<p>
The irrelevance of philosophy to religion is affirmed already by the
Khazar king in the dialogue between him and the philosopher at the
beginning of the first treatise. A prominent place in the
philosopher&rsquo;s opening presentation is reserved for the
characterization of the godhead as a static and impersonal entity,
whose relationship with the world is a necessary and indirect one. The
God of the philosopher lacks will, knowledge of particulars, interest
in human beings and reaction to their prayers and actions. The service
of God is a necessary condition for attaining human perfection. Yet it
is not related to God Himself and does not express His will.
Consequently, it follows that, from the philosophical point of view,
the varying practices of the service of God within the different
religions are of equal value (1:1).</p>

<p>
In his reaction to this presentation, the Khazar king emphasizes that
although he judges the philosopher&rsquo;s characterization of the
godhead and his estimation of religion to be convincing, it is
irrelevant to him. The Khazar king received a directive from above in
his dream to seek the desirable action in the service of God. He
considered this internal occurrence as a kind of prophetic message
directed at him. He is therefore obliged to seek the correct,
exclusive path in the service of God. From the philosopher&rsquo;s
description he has learned that philosophy has nothing to contribute
to his search (1:2). The Khazar king&rsquo;s response reveals a
fideistic basic position, that the philosophical theory of the godhead
and religion is insufficient to support and sustain a binding and
exclusive religious practice for the worshippers.</p>

<p>
The irrelevance of the philosopher for the Khazar king is not only a
matter of the doctrines and practices he has presented to him. In the
concluding section of the dialogue between them, the king also points
to the apparent evidence that prophecy and miraculous happenings
associated with it are not to be found among philosophers, while
appearing among people who have not undergone philosophical training
and have not acquired its sciences (1:4). This historical evidence
supplements the conclusion reached by the Khazar king earlier, that
for him, as one who is seeking a path originating in a semi-prophetic
revelation, philosophy and its doctrines are irrelevant.</p>

<h2 id="SkepServCritPhilKuza">5. Skepticism in the Service of the Critique of Philosophy in <em>The Kuzari</em></h2>

<p>
The philosophy laid down by the philosopher in the opening section of
<em>The Kuzari</em> represents a dogmatic approach, formulating his
claims as conclusive ones. Most philosophical schools that arose in
the ancient Greek and Hellenistic cultures were dogmatic. Alongside
them, schools with a skeptical orientation also appeared and developed
(Vogt, 2022). The proponents of these schools held the view that
philosophical discussion is a constant search that cannot be
exhausted: true philosophical discourse is characterized by its open
ongoing deliberations that avoid decisive conclusions. Loyal to this
approach, a skeptical philosopher always refrains from jumping to
conclusions. He questions the conclusions reached by representatives
of the dogmatic philosophical schools, but without attempting to
replace them with decisive conclusions of his own.</p>

<p>
Halevi&rsquo;s critique of philosophy in <em>The Kuzari</em> is based
on a fideistic assumption, not on a skeptical one. Classical
skepticism rejects dogmatic positions across the board, while Halevi
advocates certain dogmatic positions which he considers the absolute
truths of the religion of Israel. In the spirit of the fideistic
approach, he argues that these truths do not need the measures of
rational inquiry for their justification.</p>

<p>
The general culture in which Halevi operated was the classical
Islamicate culture, in which Jews like him took part alongside members
of other religions, primarily Muslims and Christians. The adherents of
these religions advocated the absolute truths of their faiths.
Similarly, the philosophical currents that appeared in this culture
were dogmatic in their tendency. Turning to skeptical philosophy and
using its measures were far from common. Against this background,
<em>The Kuzari</em> stands out as a work that utilizes a variety of
skeptical measures within a fideistic framework. Thus, Halevi&rsquo;s
use of these measures is an instrumental one. He did not seek to
establish skepticism as an alternative philosophy to the dominant
dogmatic philosophy of his time. Instead, by using skeptical measures
he strives to show that the presumption of dogmatic philosophy to
establish, by means of rational inquiry, truths that are equivalent to
truths of religion is baseless.</p>

<p>
In the spirit of classical skepticism, Halevi points out in various
places throughout <em>The Kuzari</em> that following the path of
rational inquiry cannot lead to the establishment of truths relevant
for one who believes in the Torah of Israel and accepts the yoke of
its commandments. In the spirit of the fideistic approach he adheres
to, he points out that the certainty granted to the truths of the
Jewish religion is granted to them through ways that are not the ways
of philosophy (see the discussion below).</p>

<p>
Halevi had no direct acquaintance with the literature of the skeptical
schools of the ancient Hellenistic world. The works of authors from
these schools were not translated into Arabic, unlike the works of
some representatives of the other Greek and Hellenistic philosophical
schools, primarily works by Aristotle and his commentators. The many
works translated into Arabic of the illustrations Hellenistic
physician Galen (d. c. 200), served probably, as a main source for
Halevi&rsquo;s exposure to skeptical arguments (Krinis 2020, 15,
67&ndash;69, 94). The indirect and fragmented acquaintance of authors
in the classical Islamicate culture with the Greek-Hellenistic
skeptical legacy is reflected in the sporadic, partial, and
unsystematic use that Halevi makes of measures originating from this
legacy. Nevertheless, the application he makes of several skeptical
measures is of great interest. In this application, Halevi found a way
to polemicize against philosophy, not only by means taken from
non-philosophical traditions, but also by those taken from the
inventory of the philosophical tradition itself.</p>

<p>
Moreover, the combination of moderate, selective fideism and the
variety of skeptical measures specified below is what allows Halevi to
bring supporting evidence for the claim that there is no contradiction
between the doctrines and commandments of the Torah of Israel and
human reason (1:67; 1:89). The absence of such contradiction is a
consequence of the fact that the fields in which the use of the
measures of rational inference leads to valid and certain conclusions
are irrelevant to the religion of Israel. In contrast, to the extent
that the philosophers&rsquo; use of rational measures in other fields,
such as physics and metaphysics, leads to conclusions that contradict
the doctrines and commandments unique to the Torah of Israel, these
conclusions turn out to be uncertain and doubtful. Thus, the status of
those doctrines and commandments, as ones that are neither contrary to
reason nor confirmed by reason, remains intact (2:48; 3:23; 3:53).</p>

<h3 id="DisaAmonPhil">5.1. Disagreement Among Philosophers</h3>

<p>
Disagreement (G. <em>diaph&#333;nia</em>; A. <em>l&#257; ittif&#257;q
bayna; lan muttfiq&#299;n</em>) is one of the basic measures of the
ancient skeptics in their confrontation with the representatives of
the dogmatic philosophical schools of their period. For skeptics, the
inability of dogmatic philosophers to reach agreement among themselves
on almost any issue under discussion, while applying the measures of
rational argumentation, is highly suggestive. It testifies against the
fundamental dogmatic position that the application of these measures
leads to a proven and decisive conclusion on the issues under
discussion.</p>

<p>
In Halevi&rsquo;s hands, the claim regarding disagreement among
philosophers serves as a criterion by which one can draw a boundary
line that distinguishes between the fields in which the
philosophers&rsquo; use of the measures of rational inquiry leads to
valid conclusions, and the fields in which the use of these measures
falls short of establishing such valid conclusions.</p>

<p>
According to Halevi&rsquo;s Jewish sage in <em>The Kuzari</em>, the
agreement prevailing among philosophers in the fields of mathematical
sciences and logic indicates the validity of the use of philosophical
measures of inquiry in these fields. In contrast, the disagreements
prevailing among philosophers on issues of the science of physics, and
even more so on issues of the science of metaphysics, indicate the
inability of philosophical measures to establish valid conclusions in
the domains of these sciences (5:14). This particular use that Halevi
makes of the measures of disagreement among philosophers complies with
his fideistic approach. By raising it he seeks to limit the
applicability of philosophical methods to sciences that are
inconsequential to theology and religion in terms of the knowledge
drives from them.</p>

<p>
Another application of the skeptical measure of disagreement among
philosophers concerns what the Jewish sage calls the &ldquo;the
syllogistic, governmental religion.&rdquo; In the context of the
discussion on this issue (1:12&ndash;13), this refers to the sum of
actions and beliefs necessary for the establishment of a
philosophical-political constitution with religious characteristics.
The complete failure, according to the Jewish sage, of the
philosophers to reach agreement among themselves regarding any detail
of these actions and beliefs is a highly significant failure. It shows
that the philosophers&rsquo; claim, expressed in the exchange between
the philosopher and the Khazar king (1:1&ndash;3) &ndash; to establish
a constitution based on reason, called &ldquo;the religion of the
philosophers,&rdquo; as an alternative to the revealed
religions&mdash;is a failed and false enterprise.</p>

<h3 id="PoinLackClarObsc">5.2 Pointing to Lack of Clarity and to Obscurity</h3>

<p>
The assertion that certain discussion is lacking clarity (G.
<em>ad&#275;los</em>; A. <em>aghm&#257;&#7827;, yaghmu&#7827;u</em>)
recurs very frequently in ancient skeptic literature. Since the rule
held by the skeptic philosopher is that he does not assent on unclear
issues, his pointing out that a particular issue is lacking clarity
allows him to refrain from assent on any conclusion regarding it.</p>

<p>
Halevi employs this skeptical tactic in several places in <em>The
Kuzari</em>. In the Jewish sage&rsquo;s concluding discussion on the
issue of justifying God&rsquo;s ways (theodicy)&mdash;the issue of the
reasons for &ldquo;a righteous person suffering misfortune and a
wicked person enjoying good fortune&rdquo;&mdash;he states that it is
too deep and obscure to be clarified. Accordingly, it is doubtful
whether inquiry into it can lead to the discovery of its causes
(5:21&mdash; reading here with the Oxford-Bodleian Pococke 284
manuscript, rejecting Baneth and Ben-Shammai amendment in their
critical edition, 225, n. 18). On the issue of the eternity of the
world or its creation in time, the Jewish sage states that it is deep
and obscure. This statement serves as a preliminary step to reaching
the conclusion that rational inquiry cannot lead to a conclusion on
the matter (1:67).</p>

<p>
The claim that an issue is obscure and deep also allows the Jewish
sage to refrain from deciding on the issue of anthropomorphism, as it
arises from the descriptions of God as one who directly created
material objects such as the Tablets of the Covenant. In the
discussion that develops between the Khazar king and the Jewish sage
on this issue (1:88&ndash;91), the sage initially presents an
explanation consistent with the accepted position in the philosophy
and the rationalistic theology of the period (1:89). According to this
dogmatic position, adherence to the principle of denying God&rsquo;s
corporality requires us to firmly deny the possibility of such a
direct creation. However, in his concluding remarks, the Jewish sage
insists that considering the depth and obscurity of the issue under
discussion, the explanation he previously presented to the Khazar king
should not be considered final and decisive. His concluding claim in
this discussion is that the way of creating material objects like the
Tablets of the Covenant could be clear for the ones who were direct
witnesses to such act of creation. Contrawise, its clarification by
means of rational inquiry, the method adopted by the philosophers,
cannot free us from doubts (1:91).</p>

<h3 id="EquiCoun">5.3 Equipollence of Counterarguments</h3>

<p>
Pointing to the &ldquo;equipollence or equivalence of
counterarguments&rdquo; (G. <em>isostheneia</em>; A.
<em>dal&#257;&#702;il al-&#7717;ujjatayin mutak&#257;fi&#702;a</em> is
one of the distinct measures of the skeptic discussion, in which the
skeptic philosopher presents counterarguments to the arguments of the
dogmatic philosopher. He is using this measure not to refute the
dogmatist&rsquo;s position on the issue under discussion, but to
indicate a state in which the arguments in favor of the different
positions balance each other in a way that prevents a decision between
them. In skeptic philosophy, the conclusion resulting from reaching a
state of equipollence of counterarguments is the &ldquo;suspension of
judgment&rdquo; (<em>epoch&#275;</em>).</p>

<p>
For Halevi, whose recourse to skeptical measures is derived from a
fideistic point of view, supplementing the measure of equipollence of
counterarguments with the additional skeptical measure of the
suspending judgment is unnecessary. From his perspective, a
rational-philosophical discussion on a certain theological issue that
has reached a stalemate can be resolved by resorting to ways other
than the rational-philosophical ones.</p>

<h4 id="EquiCounIssuArguDesi">5.3.1 Equipollence of Counterarguments on the Issue of the Argument from Design</h4>

<p>
In the opening part of the dialogue between the Jewish sage and the
Khazar king (1:11&ndash;25), the equipollence of counterarguments
appears only implicitly, disguised by an allegory. Moreover, the
theological issue under discussion is also disguised by means of that
same allegory. The starting point here is the question formulated by
the Jewish sage: does what is revealed about the justice and good
qualities in the conduct of the people of India necessitate honoring
and praising the name of their ruler (1:19)? Behind this statement
about the &ldquo;ruler of India&rdquo; and the just order revealed in
the conduct of his subjects stands a theological issue that the Khazar
king and the Jewish sage touched upon briefly earlier in their
exchange. This exchange revolved around the idea of religion based on
rational inquiry as a religion relying, among other things, on the
proof that God fashioned the world and governs it (1:12&ndash;13).
From this context, it can be understood that the allegory of the ruler
of India and the question asked about him are directed at the rational
proof known as the &ldquo;Argument from Design.&rdquo; According to
this argument, the order and regularities prevailing in the world are
proof of God&rsquo;s existence and His active role as the originator
of this order.</p>

<p>
In the discussion on the allegory that the Jewish sage presents about
the ruler of India and his subjects, the Argument from Design is
questioned. The Khazar king, for his part, does not deviate from the
realm of the allegory and chooses not to respond to the Jewish
sage&rsquo;s question by means of a decision&mdash;i.e., with a
positive or negative answer. Rather, he hints that this is an issue
for which several alternative answers exist, and one cannot determine
which of them is the right one: the good and just order prevailing
among the people of India may originate in themselves; it may
originate in their ruler; and it may originate in both them and their
ruler (1:20). The transition from the level of the allegory to the
level of the lesson&mdash;which Halevi does not provide but leaves to
the reader&rsquo;s understanding&mdash;indicates that the discussion
by means of rational arguments concerning the Argument from Design
ends in a state of equipollence of counterarguments. That is to say, a
state in which none of the various alternatives at stake prevail over
the others.</p>

<p>
The continuation of the exchange between the Jewish sage and the
Khazar king from this point in their dialogue turns toward the
fideistic direction. The two speakers reach the conclusion here that
although the establishment of God&rsquo;s attributes, such as Him
being the world&rsquo;s governor, cannot be reached by means of
rational inquiry, it can be reached based on the historical testimony
of God&rsquo;s exceptional involvement in the history of the people of
Israel (1:21&ndash;25).</p>

<h4 id="EquiCounIssuEterWorlVersCreaTime">5.3.2 Equipollence of Counterarguments on the Issue of the Eternity of the World Versus its Creation in Time</h4>

<p>
While the first appearance of the skeptical measure regarding the
equipollence of counterarguments in <em>The Kuzari</em> is implicit
and disguised, its next appearance, in the context of the issue of the
eternity of the world versus its creation in time, is explicit. The
concluding remarks of the Jewish sage in the discussion that takes
place between him and the Khazar king on this issue (1:43&ndash;67)
are the clearest and most concentrated example of Halevi&rsquo;s
fideistic skepticism in <em>The Kuzari</em>. These remarks include, in
a single sentence, both types of skeptical assertion, about the
obscurity of this issue and about the equipollence of the
counterarguments of eternity and of creation in time. In this context,
Halevi uses the Arabic formulation <em>dal&#257;&#702;il
al-&#7717;ujjatayin mutak&#257;fi&#702;a</em> (&ldquo;the two
arguments counterbalance one another&rdquo;), reminiscent of the term
<em>tak&#257;fu&#702; al-adillah</em> which is the Arabic equivalent
of Greek term <em>isostheneia</em> (Heck, 2013, 15, 75&ndash;81.
197&ndash;198).</p>

<p>
The assertion, supported by two skeptical measures, that the
discussion on the issue of the world&rsquo;s eternity versus its
creation in time cannot be decided by means of rational arguments,
allows the Jewish sage to transfer the discussion on the issue to the
track of a fideistic discussion. He goes on to argue that the decision
in favor of the creation of the world in time&mdash;although not
necessarily creation <em>ex nihilo</em>&mdash;is achieved by relying
on the tradition transmitted from Adam to Moses, a tradition supported
by prophecy (1:67).</p>

<h4 id="EquiCounIssuExplProcGiviFormSubs">5.3.3 Equipollence of Counterarguments on the Issue of Explaining the Process of Giving Forms to Substances</h4>

<p>
In the discussion between the Jewish sage and the Khazar king at the
opening of the fifth treatise of <em>The Kuzari</em> (5:2&ndash;6),
Halevi reconstructs two philosophical doctrines known from the
contemporary Islamicate philosophical literature. These doctrines
offer different explanations for the process of giving forms to
substances in the mundane world, which is the world beneath the lunar
sphere according to theocentric cosmological theory (Davidson 1992,
76&ndash;82, 182&ndash;183). The differences between the two
explanations under discussion are summarized in the query whether this
process results from a super-intelligent entity or proceeds on its own
without the intervention of such an entity.</p>

<p>
The first explanation emphasizes the aspect of &ldquo;chance,&rdquo;
according to which the determining factor in the issue under
discussion is what Aristotelian physics refers to as the
&ldquo;natural place&rdquo; of each of the four elements (fire, air,
water, earth) in the space between the circumference and the center of
the world beneath the lunar sphere. According to the second
explanation, the forms given to mundane substances emanate from an
entity called &ldquo;the Giver of Forms.&rdquo; This elevated entity
is identified with the super-intelligent entity of the Active
Intellect.</p>

<p>
The discussion on the issue, which initially proceeds on a purely
philosophical course (5:2&ndash;4), takes a theological turn when the
Khazar king adopts the second explanation but chooses to identify the
super-intelligent entity of the &ldquo;Giver of Forms&rdquo; not with
the Active Intellect of the philosophers, but with God (5:5). In
response, the Jewish sage shifts the discussion to the course of the
skeptical-fideistic perspective. First, he states that those who point
to the wisdom of a super-intelligent entity, either God or the Active
Intellect, as the causing factor of the giving of forms, as well as
those who entrust this task to &ldquo;chance,&rdquo; i.e. to the
natural place of the elements, can both provide a satisfactory
explanation for all the formal changes occurring in the mundane world.
That is to say, the discussion on the issue has reached the state of
equipollence of counterarguments, or as the Jewish sage comments to
the Khazar king, &ldquo;your opponent is on equal footing with
you&rdquo; (5:6).</p>

<p>
From the Jewish sage&rsquo;s point of view, the way to decide in favor
of an explanation that attributes to God the giving of forms to
substances lies in switching from the philosophical discourse to the
fideistic discourse. For him, the decisive proof on the issue under
discussion can be found in the exceptional history of the people of
Israel, where the multitude of miracles involving the change of the
forms of substances points to the existence of a divine wisdom
operating in the giving of forms to all the substances in the mundane
world (5:6).</p>

<h3 id="CastDoubPhilDoct">5.4 Casting Doubts on Philosophical Doctrines</h3>

<p>
The measure of casting doubt is also integrated into the skeptical
rationalist strategy that Halevi employs in a substantial number of
places in the <em>The Kuzari</em>, when he argues against different
doctrines of the philosophy of his time. Like the other skeptical
measures, the measure of casting doubt is not aimed at establishing
and proving a counterargument to the particular dogmatic philosophical
argument it calls into question. The effectiveness of casting doubt
lies in its pointing to weaknesses in the arguments that dogmatic
philosophers bring to establish their doctrines. The use of this
measure seeks to undermine the philosophers&rsquo; claim that their
arguments constitute decisive proofs of those doctrines.</p>

<h4 id="CastDoubDoctSepaInte">5.4.1 Casting Doubts on the Doctrine of the Separate Intellects</h4>

<p>
A common philosophical conception in Halevi&rsquo;s time, expressed by
the philosopher summoned to the Khazar king&rsquo;s court, identifies
God as the First Cause of the world&rsquo;s existence (1:1). However,
the world is characterized by the multiplicity and diversity of its
beings, while God, according to the current philosophical terminology,
is &ldquo;One,&rdquo; meaning a separate, unified entity that cannot
be multiplied or divided. The question therefore arises: how can the
One serve as the first cause for a world of multiple beings? The
philosophical solution to this problem, to which the Jewish sage draws
attention, is the solution that relies on the principle that
&ldquo;from the One, only one proceeds&rdquo; (4:25). According to the
philosophical explanation based on this principle, by knowing Himself
as the First Cause, God emanates only one separate rational being. The
multiplicity of beings is therefore the result of the continuation of
the process that evolves from the emanation of that first being.</p>

<p>
The one being that emanated from God, known as the First Separate
Intellect, knows not only itself but also its Divine Cause.
Accordingly, two beings emanate from it: another separate intellect
and the highest sphere of the celestial world. The subsequent process
of emanation of separate intellects and celestial spheres continues
until the emanation of the lowest separate intellect, called the
Active Intellect, and the parallel emanation of the mundane world, the
world beneath the lunar sphere.</p>

<p>
Basing himself on al-Ghazali (Wolfson 1977a, 11&ndash;13), Halevi
casts doubt on this philosophical theory by raising several
difficulties it entails. First, the way in which the process of
emanation originating in God stops with the emanation of the Active
Intellect creates an unexplained limitation on God&rsquo;s ability.
Second, since the creative principle in the emanation process is that
of the emanated beings knowing themselves and the beings above them,
this process should have led to a multiplicity of beings in the
celestial world, far beyond the creation of one separate intellect and
sphere at each and every stage of the described emanation process.
Furthermore, according to the same creative principle, the
philosopher, having perfected himself, should have emanated a sphere
as one who knows himself, and a separate intellect as one who knows
God, the First Cause, (4:25; 5:14).</p>

<p>
In casting doubt by pointing to specific weaknesses in the
philosophical emanation theory, Halevi seeks to undermine the
confidence of those among his readers who were attracted to that
philosophical doctrine. Beyond this, his fundamental critique of the
philosophical doctrine of Separate Intellects is rooted in the
author&rsquo;s religious worldview. That critique appears in the final
and concluding discussion that the Jewish sage dedicates to this
doctrine. His main claim in this discussion is that the philosophical
emanation doctrine &ldquo;prompted the philosophers to multiply [the
number of] deities.&rdquo; This multiplicity infringes upon the unity
and sovereignty of God to the extent that it makes the one who is
tempted to accept this doctrine a heretic (5:21).</p>

<h4 id="CastDoubDoctImmaImmoRatiSoul">5.4.2 Casting Doubt on the Doctrine of the Immateriality and Immortality of the Rational Soul</h4>

<p>
Another topic that serves as a target for Halevi&rsquo;s skeptic
measure of casting doubt is the philosophical doctrine concerning the
immateriality and immortality of the rational soul. Ibn Sina&rsquo;s
version of this doctrine is presented (anonymously) by the Jewish sage
in a section of the fifth treatise of <em>The Kuzari</em> (5:12).</p>

<p>
The Jewish sage supplements his presentation of Ibn Sina&rsquo;s
doctrine of the soul by casting doubt on the part of it related to the
rational soul (5:14). His critique is divided into two parts. First,
it focuses on the philosophical assertion that the rational soul is an
immaterial substance, and therefore the processes of generation and
corruption that affect material substances do not affect it. In this
context, the Jewish sage mentions a list of ailments and diseases that
afflict human beings, including philosophers&mdash;drunkenness, fever,
a blow that damages the brain, old age and senility, melancholy, and
mental illness. Each of these afflictions can cause the affected
person to forget the knowledge he has acquired and to change his
personality. These occurrences, which human life is prone to, cast
doubt onthe philosophers&rsquo; claim regarding the immunity of the
rational soul from material processes of decay.</p>

<p>
Then the Jewish sage moves on to cast doubt on the assertion about the
immortality of the rational soul, which is derived from the
philosophical assertion that this particular soul can achieve a status
which makes it independent of bodily forces. In this context, the
Jewish sage raises a difficulty regarding the scope of knowledge that
the one who perfects himself in the philosophical sciences must
acquire so that his rational soul will attain independence from the
bodily forces and immortality upon the destruction of his bodily
limbs. One possibility is that achieving this status requires
acquiring full and comprehensive knowledge. If so, then this is a goal
that no one can achieve, since even the philosopher cannot acquire
knowledge about everything that exists in the heavens, on earth, and
in the sea. The other possibility is that achieving the immortality of
the rational soul requires acquiring only partial knowledge. If so,
then this is a relatively easy goal to achieve, which many can
complete in a short time. This possibility is problematic from the
philosophers&rsquo; elitist point of view.</p>

<p>
In this case as well, the measure of casting doubt is intended to
undermine the readers&rsquo; confidence in the validity of this
philosophical doctrine and not much more. Faithful to his
fideistic-skeptical approach, Halevi concludes the discussion with the
Jewish sage&rsquo;s assertion that the issue of the rational
soul&rsquo;s immateriality and immortality is one of the theological
issues that cannot be decided by means of rational inquiry. Those who
debate about it in this manner would waste their lives in vain,
without succeeding in reaching a decisive conclusion about it. In
Halevi&rsquo;s opinion, the Jewish reader does not need the doubtful
proofs of philosophy about the soul&rsquo;s immateriality and
immortality. He should rely on what the tradition of his religion
provides him on this matter, since this tradition was transmitted by
reliable transmitters (5:14).</p>

<h4 id="CastDoubDoctFourElem">5.4.3 Casting Doubt on the Doctrine of the Four Elements</h4>

<p>
The Jewish sage finds it appropriate to cast doubt on the
philosophical doctrine of the four elements &ndash; fire, air, water,
and earth &ndash; which serve as building blocks of all the material
substances in the mundane world. The doubt that the Jewish sage raises
in this matter concerns the need to empirically verify this physical
doctrine. The Jewish sage&rsquo;s position is that the empirical test
confirms the philosophical doctrine regarding the role of the four
primary qualities&mdash; hotness, coldness, wetness, and dryness
&mdash;in the processes of generation and corruption of material
substances. We can identify, by means of empirical observation, the
existence and involvement of these four qualities in these
processes.</p>

<p>
In contrast, the empirical tests do not confirm the philosophical
doctrine regarding the role reserved for the four elements in those
processes. Our observational attempts to capture and diagnose the
appearance of these elements in their purity, during the operation of
the processes of generation and corruption in nature, are destined to
fail, except perhaps in rare cases, too rare to generalize from them
sweeping theoretical conclusions as the philosophers do (5:14).</p>

<p>
In the continuation of the same discussion, the Jewish sage creates a
connection between the doctrine of the four elements and a
philosophical doctrine with clear theological implications, that of
the eternity of the world. The connection between these two doctrines
suggests that the processes of generation and corruption of matter in
the mundane world are eternal, without a starting or ending point.
Hence, the existence of the four elements in a pure and separate
manner has never actually happened and never will. And if so, then the
assumption regarding their separate existence is not susceptible to
observational verification. It must therefore be treated as a purely
theoretical matter whose actual existence is doubtful.</p>

<p>
The Jewish sage concludes the discussion on the issue under discussion
in a manner consistent with the fideistic approach that characterizes
Halevi&rsquo;s attitude toward philosophical issues with theological
implications. He directs his interlocutor, the Khazar king, to the
opinion he calls the &ldquo;opinion of the Divine Law.&rdquo;
According to this opinion, the mundane world with all its objects and
the forms given to them was created by God at the time He wished, and
in the manner that He wished. Accepting this opinion will spare the
Khazar king and the readers of <em>The Kuzari</em> the trouble of
toiling in the inquiry of the generation of material substances, as
well as the inquiry of related and other issues (5:14). At the same
time, adherence to what is identified here as the &ldquo;opinion of
the Divine Law&rdquo; saves the author of <em>The Kuzari</em> the need
to provide alternative explanations to those presented by the
philosophers in their discussions of these issues (Langermann, 1997,
509&ndash;512).</p>

<p>
In the same section from the fifth treatise of the dialogue in which
the Jewish sage casts doubt on various philosophical doctrines, he
also supports his efforts to do so using a claim of a different order,
essential in nature. This claim, which he first presented in the
book&rsquo;s first treatise, regards the unbridgeable
hierarchical-ontological difference between the chosen of
humanity&mdash;among whom are those who have reached prophecy and
those who have the potential to be prophets&mdash;and all those
unchosen human beings, who, due to their lower status on the
hierarchical scale of creatures in the mundane world, lack the ability
to reach the degree of prophecy.</p>

<p>
In the first treatise, where this claim is presented in detail
(1:27;1:31&ndash;43; 1:47; 1:95; 1:103; 1:15), it does not refer to
the philosophers (Krinis, 2014). However, in its abbreviated
appearance in the fifth treatise, it is directed toward them.
According to what the Jewish sage has to say here, philosophers&rsquo;
shortcomings stem not only from limitations of the rational measures,
but also from their inferior position on the hierarchical scale. The
mere human nature of the philosophers, who are explicitly identified
throughout the book as Greeks, limits them and does not allow them to
achieve what is directly accessible only to those human beings whose
nature is not limited to the human degree, but includes a supra-human
component that opens their way to prophecy (5:14).</p>

<h2 id="SignGodsPresWaysPercThem">6. Signs of God&rsquo;s Presence and the Ways to Perceive Them</h2>

<p>
The issue of the signs of God&rsquo;s presence and the ways to
perceive and experience these signs stands at the center of <em>The
Kuzari</em>. Halevi&rsquo;s uniqueness and greatness as a religious
thinker rests to a large extent on the emphasis he gave to this issue
in his thought. The Jewish thinkers and authors preceding him and his
contemporaries were not particularly troubled by this issue, but for
Halevi, this is the main point.</p>

<p>
In his understanding, there is a huge gap that requires explanation
between the multitude of manifestations of Divine Presence, such as
miracles and prophecy, in the distant past of the people of Israel,
and the almost complete absence of these manifestations in the current
period, the prolonged period of exile of the people from the Holy
Land. This gap is the great common theme of his poetry and thought. In
many places in his poetry, Halevi gave an intensified emotional
expression to the pain and frustration he felt in the face of the gap
between the glory of the people of Israel in the distant past, when
signs of their closeness to God were abundant, and the suffering and
humiliation that are the lot of the people in the reality of exile,
when manifestations of God&rsquo;s presence are almost completely
absent (Alfonso 2008, 94&ndash;96). In <em>The Kuzari</em>, he seeks
not only to point out this sharp gap but also to diagnose it, in a way
that points to a direction that may lead to restoration of the
manifestations of God&rsquo;s presence in the history of people of
Israel.</p>

<p>
The prominent feature in <em>The Kuzari</em> that attests to the
special importance that Halevi attributes to the issue of the signs of
God&rsquo;s presence, and the possibility of perceiving and
experiencing them, is the frequent appearance throughout the book of
the term &ldquo;the Divine Thing&rdquo; (<em>al-amr
al-il&#257;h&#299;</em>). As far as Jewish thought is concerned, the
use of this term is an innovation of Halevi. It does not appear in the
works of his predecessors and contemporaries, certainly not in a
systematic manner. In <em>The Kuzari</em>, however, it is the central
term. Its centrality in the book stems from the fact that Halevi chose
it as an all-encompassing term to denote the Divine Presence. This
Divine Presence is expressed in a variety of phenomena that
distinguish the existence of God&rsquo;s chosen and of the people of
Israel as the chosen people of God from the existence of the rest of
the human beings and the nations (Davidson 1972, 381&ndash;395;
Wolfson 1977b, 68&ndash;85, Pines, 1980, 165&ndash;192; Lobel, 2006;
Krinis 2014, 189&ndash;223).</p>

<h3 id="InadPhilEpisPercSignGodsPres">6.1 The Inadequacy of Philosophical Epistemology in Perceiving the Signs of God&rsquo;s Presence</h3>

<p>
The centrality reserved in Halevi&rsquo;s thought for the experience
of the manifestations of God&rsquo;s presence has a far-reaching
implication for his critique of philosophy. The claim that Halevi
formulates in <em>The Kuzari</em> is that the rationalistic
epistemology that was best developed and refined by the philosophers
is not the appropriate way to perceive and experience the signs of
God&rsquo;s presence (4:3).</p>

<p>
In Halevi&rsquo;s understanding, the signs of God&rsquo;s presence are
sensory signs, as in the case of miracles, or perceptible signs as in
the case of prophecy. Furthermore, the manifestations of the Divine
Presence are concrete and unique manifestations. They occur in
specific historical circumstances and not in others and are
experienced by specific individuals and groups and not by others, and
in specific places. God is in the details, yet the philosophers seek
to find Him in the universal: in the regularities of the nature when
it comes to physics, and in claims with general validity regarding the
relations between God and the world when comes to metaphysics. Thus,
philosophers&rsquo; epistemology does not account for anything that is
concrete, unique, and circumstantial in God&rsquo;s presence in the
world.</p>

<p>
In the previous part of our discussion, we saw that Halevi, in
accordance with his fideistic-skeptical approach, casts doubt on and
minimizes the range of efficacy of the philosophical methods of
inference in achieving the universal goals they set for themselves.
What is not in doubt, according to him, is that the rationalistic
epistemology of the philosophers is unsuitable and ineffective
regarding the achievement of the truly sublime goal from the religious
point of view that interests him: the goal of perceiving and
experiencing the signs of God&rsquo;s presence in the world.</p>

<p>
Halevi emphasizes the inadequacy of philosophical epistemology in the
interpretation he gives to the difference between two biblical names
of God: the name <em>Elohim</em> and the explicit name (YHVH &ndash;
the Tetragrammaton). The optimal use that the philosophers made of the
methods of rational inference granted them the kind of apprehension of
God symbolized by the name <em>Elohim</em>. This apprehension does
point to God as the force that brings reality into existence by virtue
of His being the first cause of this reality (1:1). It may also lead
to the conclusion that God is the establisher of the order prevailing
in the world (4:15). However, this picture is of a Deity whose essence
is exalted and elevated above the world and is not at all accessible
to its inhabitants (1:1; 1:8; 1:68; 4:1). Such a God is a Deity whose
presence is distant, indirect and mediated, to the extent that He is
one &ldquo;who neither harms us nor benefits us, knows nothing of our
prayers and sacrifices, and nothing of our obedience and
disobedience&rdquo; (4:3).</p>

<p>
In contrast, the Tetragrammaton, as God&rsquo;s personal name,
symbolizes the apprehension of His direct presence in the reality of
the lives of His chosen ones. For example, Adam, as the first of
God&rsquo;s chosen ones, would not apprehend God&mdash;that is,
experience the signs of His presence, &ldquo;had He not addressed him,
rewarded him, punished him, and created Eve for him from one of his
own ribs&rdquo; (4:3). Similarly, God&rsquo;s chosen among the
descendants of Adam were granted knowledge of God by His personal
name, because they alone experienced the direct signs of His presence,
primarily prophecy (4:3; 4:15). At a later stage, the people of Israel
were privileged to be called &ldquo;the people of YHVH,&rdquo; because
the signs of His presence appeared and were experienced in the sphere
of their history, and not in the sphere of the history of the other
nations (2:32; 4:3).</p>

<h3 id="WaysLeadPercSignGodsPres">6.2 The Ways that Lead to Perceiving the Signs of God&rsquo;s Presence</h3>

<p>
Judah Halevi&rsquo;s interpretation regarding the distinct difference
between the apprehension of God symbolized by the name <em>Elohim</em>
and His apprehension symbolized by His explicit personal name relies
on the claim regarding the exclusive appearance of the signs of
God&rsquo;s presence in the course of the history of God&rsquo;s
chosen ones and chosen people (Harvey, 1988; Harvey, 2007; Ben-Sasson,
2019, 151&ndash;158). This claim, in turn, rests on the fundamental
hierarchical-ontological distinction that points to an unbridgeable
gap between the degree of God&rsquo;s chosen ones and the degree of
the unchosen among all of humanity (4:15).</p>

<p>
However, for Halevi, as far as the critique of philosophy is
concerned, it is not this aspect but the epistemological aspect of his
interpretation of God&rsquo;s names that more fruitfully reflects the
difference and tension between the God of Israel and the God of the
philosophers. In this context, Halevi points to two alternative ways
to that of philosophical rational epistemology, by means of which the
signs of the Divine Presence can be perceived.</p>

<p>
The first way is based on outwardly directed sensory apprehension.
This way is open to the entire chosen people and concerns the
appearance of the signs of God&rsquo;s presence in public-historical
settings. The second way is based on inwardly directed senses. It is
relevant for chosen individuals and concerns the mystical-prophetic
apprehension of God&rsquo;s presence.</p>

<h4 id="ApprSignGodsPresThroSensTrad">6.2.1 Apprehending the Signs of God&rsquo;s Presence Through the Senses and Tradition</h4>

<p>
When the Khazar king summons the Jewish sage to his court, he asks him
to present the core principles of his faith. Responding to this
request, the Jewish sage points primarily to the signs of the Divine
Presence in the history of the people of Israel, as they were revealed
in the miracles accompanying the events of the Exodus from Egypt, the
mission of Moses at the giving of the Torah, and the great number of
prophets who arose from the people of Israel following Moses
(1:11).</p>

<p>
This presentation by the sage of Judaism&rsquo;s principles of faith
disappoints the Khazar king. He expected to hear a presentation of the
core principles of a religion, composed of universal truths such as
God being &ldquo;the Creator of the world, who orders it and governs
it&rdquo; (1:12)&mdash;that is, the dogmas of religion that the Jewish
sage calls the &ldquo;syllogistic, governmental religion to which
inquiry leads,&rdquo; and which the philosophers frequently engage in
(1:13).</p>

<p>
From this starting point, the discussion in the opening dialogue
between the Jewish sage and the Khazar king (1:11&ndash;25) proceeds
on two levels. The first is the critical level, where the two debaters
point to weaknesses in the &ldquo;syllogistic, governmental
religion&rdquo; based on a rationalistic epistemology. Those
weaknesses are the disagreement among philosophers about it and the
equipollence of counterarguments applicable to one of its central
proofs (see sections 5.1, 5.3 above). The second level is where the
Jewish sage formulates in great brevity an alternative epistemology to
the rational-philosophical one, one that is suitable for perceiving
and transmitting the public signs of God&rsquo;s presence in the
history of the people of Israel (Krinis 2020, 35&ndash;61).</p>

<p>
The miraculous events that happened to the people of Israel, such as
the plagues of Egypt and the parting of the Red Sea, were public
events whose tangibility is clear and impressive, to the extent that,
in the opinion of the Jewish sage, they do not require confirmation
and proof by reason. Direct sensory perception
(<em>al-&#703;iy&#257;n</em>) is sufficient to grasp and confirm their
truth for those who actually experienced them (1:11; 1:15). Since
miracles like the plagues of Egypt or the parting of the Red Sea,
exceptional as they are, are historical events, the question arises as
to the way that ensures the most reliable transmission of knowledge
about them to those who did not experience them, including those of
future generations. In this context, the Jewish sage presents the
doctrine of &ldquo;concurrent tradition&rdquo;
(<em>taw&#257;tur</em>), i.e. a report transmitted through multiple
and separate chains of transmitters (1:25; 1:89; 5:14).</p>

<p>
Traditional knowledge, that is reports passed orally from mouth to ear
and from generation to generation, held a central place in the Jewish
culture of late antiquity and in the culture of early Islam. In the
Muslim culture, knowledge transmitted across generations through
multiple and separate chains of transmitters was considered knowledge
with the highest degree of reliability (Nasser 2013, 65&ndash;78). It
is considered so because the correspondence maintained in all the
transmissions of its content, along with the multiplicity of
transmitters and the separation maintained between its chains of
transmitters, nullify the possibility that such knowledge could be
distorted through conspiracy and coordination of versions (1:48; 3:24;
3:73; 4:3; 4:11).</p>

<p>
Thus, the epistemology associated with the reception and transmission
of the signs of the Divine Presence revealed in public events includes
two complementary components: their apprehension through the direct
sensory perception of those who were direct witnesses to them; and the
transmission of their testimony to others who were not present at
these events as a &ldquo;concurrent tradition&rdquo; through multiple
and separate chains of transmitters.</p>

<p>
The differences between the epistemology relying on these two
components and rationalistic-philosophical epistemology are summarized
in the following two points. First, the type of knowledge: it is
knowledge about a specific tangible event exposed to the direct
apprehension of the senses, not knowledge consisting of universal
generalizations. Second, the method of verification of the knowledge:
verification that relies on the manner of transmission of the
knowledge through transmitters, and not on its verification by means
of rational arguments and proofs.</p>

<p>
The distinction between the two epistemologies allows the Jewish sage
to conclude the first discussion between him and the Khazar king with
his own interpretation of the first of the Ten Commandments. At the
foundational occasion of the revelation at Mount Sinai, God presented
Himself to the people of Israel, not with a declaration such as
&ldquo;I am the Creator of the world and your Creator,&rdquo; but with
the declaration &ldquo;I am God, whom you worship, who brought you out
of the land of Egypt.&rdquo; That is to say, He did not address them
by way of an ahistorical universal statement about the relationship
between Him and the world, a way of presentation that lends itself to
verification by means of rational proofs. Instead, He addressed them
by way of referring to His involvement in the miraculous historical
events that they themselves witnessed and experienced. This way of
presentation is the one that lends itself to perception and
verification by means of the combination of direct sensory experience
and faithful transmission guaranteed by the &ldquo;concurrent
tradition&rdquo; (1:25).</p>

<h4 id="PercSignGodsPresThroInteSensMystExpe">6.2.2 Perceiving the Signs of God&rsquo;s Presence Through the Internal Senses and Mystical Experience</h4>

<p>
According to Halevi&rsquo;s understanding, prophecy is the ultimate
experience of closeness to God, fear of Him, and love of Him. He was
well aware of the attempts by Muslim philosophers to explain the
phenomenon of prophecy as a possible consequence of the
philosopher&rsquo;s state of attachment to the Active Intellect,
having prepared himself and attained intellectual perfection (1:1;
1:87; 4:3; 5:14). The presumption of the philosophers of his time and
culture to provide an explanation for prophecy in terms of
intellectual perfection and relation with an intermediary entity posed
a special challenge for Halevi and elicited from him particularly
strong polemical tones.</p>

<p>
In the sayings he ascribes to the Jewish sage and the Khazar king
during their debate in the fourth treatise of <em>The Kuzari</em>,
Halevi takes great pains to show how unworthy the philosophers are of
prophecy. Following their discussion in the opening dialogue of the
first treatise, the discussion between the Jewish sage and the Khazar
king in the fourth treatise points out how the philosophers&rsquo;
focus on abstract rational thought, which strives to achieve universal
generalizations, is a highly unsuitable way for perceiving and
experiencing the signs of God&rsquo;s presence. However, within the
framework of the sage and the king&rsquo;s lengthy discussion about
prophecy, Halevi goes into greater detail in clarifying the weaknesses
of the philosophers&rsquo; path and the serious implications of their
adherence to this path from a religious point of view.</p>

<p>
The critical approach Halevi presents in this discussion can be
summarized in the following points:</p>

<ul class="jfy">

<li>Human intellectual capacity is characterized by its ability to
focus on only one thing at any given time, while prophecy, for its
part, is characterized by being a holistic apprehension in which the
prophet grasps many things at once (4:5&ndash;6).</li>

<li>Philosophical thinking strives for abstraction and thereby tries
to overcome images. However, images, whether produced by direct
sensory perceptions or assisted by imagination, are essential for
human perception across the board. In the case of prophecy, this
involves visualization assisted by imagination for the purpose of
receiving visions of the sublime Divine world. These visions are not
accessible to one who adopts the path of intellectual abstraction
(4:3&ndash;5).</li>

<li>The above-mentioned critique by Halevi of the philosophers&rsquo;
denial of the centrality of images and imagination for the religious
experience in general, and for prophecy in particular, entails a
far-reaching psychological implication. Human emotional motivation
needs visualization. In the absence of visualization, the emotions of
fear of God and love of God, which are the essential emotions for
devotion to God and for accepting the yoke of His service, will not be
aroused in human beings. Fear of God and love of Him are established
in prophets&rsquo; hearts immediately upon their experience of the
tangible, stunning experience of the prophetic vision (4:3&ndash;5).
In comparison, the philosophers&rsquo; focus on abstract thinking that
leads to the apprehension of the intelligible greatly limits their
psychological motivational capacity regarding the arousal of the
essential emotions of fear of God and love of Him. Consequently, the
philosopher&rsquo;s motivation lies not in his desire to fulfill
God&rsquo;s will, but in his effort to achieve intellectual
perfection, so that his intellect will rise to the stage of unifying
attachment with the Active Intellect. To achieve this goal, the
philosopher needs to apprehend all the intelligibles and the sciences.
Therefore, for the philosopher, the apprehension of God&rsquo;s
reality and unity is no more than the apprehension of one intelligible
among the many he needs to apprehend (1:1; 4:13; 4:19). It is no
surprise, then, that the philosopher&rsquo;s commitment to religious
law is superficial and weak. For him, this law is, at most,
&ldquo;fine manners.&rdquo; There is also an opportunistic dimension
to the philosopher&rsquo;s attitude toward religious law. He observes
this law only as long as it does not harm him and he does not suffer
difficulties because of it (4:15&ndash;16; 4:19).</li>
</ul>

<p>
The main alternative that Halevi presents to the philosophical
explanation of prophecy relies on the decisive role reserved for what
the Jewish sage calls the &ldquo;internal sense&rdquo; in the process
of prophecy. In the discussion he develops on the matter, the sage
names several terms referring to several such internal senses,
including the &ldquo;internal eye&rdquo; (<em>al-&#703;ayn
al-b&#257;&#7789;ina</em>), &ldquo;sight&rdquo;
(<em>ba&#7779;&#299;rah</em>), &ldquo;taste&rdquo; (<em>dawq</em>),
and &ldquo;vision&rdquo; (<em>mush&#257;hada</em>). These terms are
familiar from Muslim Sufi literature, where they are described not
only as internal senses but also as internal mystical states (Efros,
1941; Lobel 2000, 89&ndash;120; Harvey 2006, 292&ndash;293). As in
Sufi literature, so too in <em>The Kuzari</em>, the use of the
internal senses leads the user to a mystical experience of complete
devotion to the love of God (4:3; 4:15&ndash;16). Halevi emphasizes
that the internal sense is a special aptitude with which the prophets
were endowed. The Greek philosophers, as those who were not endowed
with such an aptitude and were forced to rely on their inferences
alone, could not perceive or confirm the tangible visions revealed to
the prophets through their internal sense (4:3).</p>

<p>
In a high point of that discussion, the Khazar king raises the
distinction between &ldquo;the God of Abraham and the God of
Aristotle.&rdquo; This distinction returns to the difference between
the apprehension of the name <em>Elohim</em> and the apprehension of
God&rsquo;s explicit personal name, i.e., the issue with which the
discussion on prophecy opened in the fourth treatise of <em>The
Kuzari</em>. Aristotle, the paragon of the philosophers, reached
through his intellect a purely theoretical apprehension only of the
name <em>Elohim</em>. Thus, his apprehension of God remained an
obscure, doubtful, and non-binding matter. For Abraham, the exemplary
believer, who was privileged to receive prophecy and the apprehension
of the God&rsquo;s explicit personal name through the internal senses,
the prophetic experience he underwent was a life-changing event. It
caused him not only to leave his father&rsquo;s household and the land
where he was born, but also to abandon the theoretical-philosophical
sciences with which he had previously occupied himself
(4:15&ndash;16).</p>

<p>
Aristotle took the way of philosophy, and Abraham took the way of
prophecy. From Halevi&rsquo;s perspective, these are two separate
ways, and only the second of them truly leads to God (4:27).</p>
</div>

